perspective on the AI journey

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.710
that has happened, and
kind of contrast it

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.850
with a couple of other
things that are going on.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.530
Now, AI, of course, has been
thought about for a long time

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:06.000
as something that can
act, pass a Turing test,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.150
and act and think
like a human being.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:13.050
Now of course, for those of you
who know, the test by itself

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:16.620
is sometimes easy to pass
and sometimes difficult.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.570
Some of you would have
seen the movie called

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:22.980
"Ex Machina," where the entire
premise was around the Turing

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.120
test.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.420
But if you think about it,
there was a computer program

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:28.485
called ELIZA.

00:02:28.485 --> 00:02:30.360
Any of you have heard
of the computer program

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.230
called ELIZA in the '60s?

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:33.240
Yes.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.360
And ELIZA proved to
me once and for all

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.970
that we pay too much money
paid to the psychiatrist,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:42.270
the $1,000 an hour,
because all ELIZA had

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.600
to do on the other end
was respond back with,

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.530
if you said that
you're feeling down,

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.990
she would say, tell me
why you're feeling down.

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.775
And there goes your $1,000.

00:02:50.775 --> 00:02:52.920
So it did appear
like a psychiatrist.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:57.090
But we know for all that she was
actually not really doing much.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:00.480
The contrast with AI,
really, is this concept

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.970
of IA, which has had a
much more profound impact

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:06.710
in our existence.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:10.332
The concept of IA, or
intelligence amplification,

00:03:10.332 --> 00:03:11.790
was brought about
by the people who

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:15.730
were doing cybernetics
in '50s and '60s

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.970
about machine and
human interaction.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:21.870
And William Ross Ashby
actually coined the term,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:25.510
where he said that
machines actually are there

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:29.350
to amplify human
intelligence, as opposed

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:32.450
to AI, whose goal it is
to actually figure out

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:36.030
whether machines can actually
substitute human intelligence.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:42.860
And if you look at this entire
IT span, the evolution of IT

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:46.210
over the last 40 years,
a huge amount of it

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:50.230
has been in terms of
intelligence amplification, as

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.860
opposed to artificial
intelligence.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:56.230
And what's happening is that
the hype, whether it's the hype

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:59.660
or whether it is the reality or
whether the fear of what else

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.770
is AI tends to dominate
the conversation.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.250
But really it is AI and IA
that kind of go together.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.960
To kind of bring
these things together,

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.390
if you think about
the Google car,

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:15.270
the Waymo car is a car
which says, doesn't even

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:19.779
have a steering wheel, does
not even have a steering wheel,

00:04:19.779 --> 00:04:22.089
whereas Tesla, on
the other hand,

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:28.850
is there supporting the human
beings make better decisions.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:30.580
Keep that in mind
as you kind of think

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:35.132
through all of this
discussion and technology

00:04:35.132 --> 00:04:36.340
when it comes to all of this.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.580
Now, what's interesting for
us, some of us geeks out there,

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:42.175
is irrespective whether
we talk about AI

00:04:42.175 --> 00:04:45.940
or whether we talk about
IA, underneath all of it

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:48.580
is machine learning.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.110
And what machine
learning really is,

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:56.070
it's all about bringing
the power of machines

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:57.840
to actually learn.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.930
And the three facets that the
machines actually learn with,

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:04.640
and Fei-Fei brought out a
fourth one called talent,

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:05.970
but I'll ignore that for now.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.770
We are all extremely
talented folks anyway,

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.530
so that's a given.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:14.550
It's data, computation,
and computation

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.890
is shown as a calculator.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:17.560
Don't know why.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:23.340
And by the way, my
favorite calculator

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.080
is the HP calculator.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.990
Anybody who talks to me
about any other calculator

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.090
is not going to go
anywhere with me.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:31.515
And then algorithms.

00:05:31.515 --> 00:05:35.170
So as you all think about
the power of AI, IA,

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.890
and machine learning,
think about what

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.490
is your currency that you have?

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:42.380
Do you have access to data?

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:44.350
Do you have access
to computation?

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.180
And do you have
access to algorithms?

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:51.769
And where you think that you
may be falling a bit short,

00:05:51.769 --> 00:05:53.810
and if you really believe
at the end of this talk

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:56.160
that it's really, really
important for AI to play

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.740
a role in it, but you're falling
short on one of these three

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.290
dimensions, what
is your strategy

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.770
to actually kind of complement
and build yourself around it?

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:10.855
Now, in order for me to
kind of connect AI and APIs,

00:06:10.855 --> 00:06:15.720
I thought I'll take you
through a journey of AI.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.150
And the best way
to take a journey

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:21.630
is when human beings want to
pit themselves against machines.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.500
Not ELIZA.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:27.610
And human beings have
almost always decided--

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:30.760
not always decided,
have felt that one

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:33.020
of the tasks that they want
to compete with machines

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:35.390
is in playing games.

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:37.160
The routes are well-defined.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:40.770
The winners and the losers
are clearly known at the end.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:42.269
So why won't we play games?

00:06:42.269 --> 00:06:44.060
So what I'm going to
do is kind of take you

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:48.590
through a journey of AI and its
technology through some games

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:52.130
that humans, in the form
of programming computation,

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:53.990
have played with
real human beings.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.460
So some of you
remember Deep Blue.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:05.430
IBM's Deep Blue computer beat,
in the rematch, Garry Kasparov.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:10.500
And some of you may have
seen Garry's expressions.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.560
He was not pleased.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:14.210
He was not pleased.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:16.400
But in reality,
people said, this

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:21.748
is the dawn of
machine intelligence.

00:07:21.748 --> 00:07:23.572
No.

00:07:23.572 --> 00:07:24.490
No.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:27.070
This was really
machine computation

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:30.010
beating human intelligence.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:33.740
The rules of chess allowed
the Deep Blue machine--

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:35.390
I was at IBM at that time--

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:39.170
to look far enough in
advance and figure out what

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:42.030
is kind of the optimal move.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:45.580
So this was not
machine intelligence.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:49.080
And then, again
when I was at IBM,

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:52.850
those of you who remember
IBM Watson playing

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:58.180
against Ken Jennings and the
other person and winning,

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:02.020
and Ken Jennings at the end
saying, "I, for one, welcome

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:05.500
my machine overlords."

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:10.270
But for those of you who
saw that, if you remember,

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:14.814
Watson laid a giant turd
at the end of the game.

00:08:14.814 --> 00:08:17.230
I don't know how many of you
remember, but for all of you,

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:20.810
I'll just kind of
bring it out for you.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:30.760
The question was, name a US city
whose largest airport is named

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:36.159
after something that happened--

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:39.039
an important person
in World War II

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:41.679
and whose second-largest
airport is named

00:08:41.679 --> 00:08:46.470
after a battle in World War II.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:48.840
And guess what
Watson came up with?

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:51.570
Toronto.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:55.240
Now, it probably ascribed
to an imperialistic vision

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:59.290
of taking over the world and
making Canada the 51st state

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:00.550
or whatever it was.

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:04.690
But you realize at
that very moment

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:09.130
that as intelligent
as computers can get,

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:12.250
they are as fallible
as human beings.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:14.950
But what was interesting
was that was a step away

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:19.210
from just computation to
actually understanding

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:19.840
language.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.900
And for those of you who
love to play "Jeopardy!",

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:25.180
understanding the
subtlety and puns

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:28.930
and everything else, and this
was definitely an advancement.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:32.842
So we went from computation to
some amount of intelligence.

00:09:32.842 --> 00:09:34.800
But what's really happening
in this world today

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:40.990
is that there's a whole ream
of new machine learning and AI

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:42.750
techniques that
are coming about.

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:44.670
And the most
promising of them is

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:46.980
what's called neural networks.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:51.350
And neural networks try
to mimic human brains,

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.420
even though human
brains are made up

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:56.460
of trillions of connections,
whereas neural networks aren't,

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:58.580
and therefore they can
solve simpler problems.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:00.770
But what neural
networks tried to do

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:03.350
is that there is an
interconnectivity

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:10.250
of computational bits, and you
give it an input on one side

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:12.350
and you tell it what
the output should be,

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:15.940
and the network then
learns by itself.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:18.590
And what it does, the
kind of magic it does,

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:23.990
the steepest gradient descent,
I loved writing the go program

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:28.070
for a three-way neural
network and everything else

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:31.750
in order to kind of learn
what the heck was going on.

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:37.280
It kind of learns, and then
when you give it something new,

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:41.180
it is able to tell you
that this is a cat.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:46.370
Now, this is in contrast with
how machine intelligence worked

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:47.320
in the past.

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:50.030
Machine intelligence
in the past,

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:53.390
you may have programmed a
rule in the end which said,

00:10:53.390 --> 00:11:00.650
if that animal has whiskers
and shows an attitude,

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:02.150
then it's a cat.

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:05.630
If an animal has whiskers and
is very loving, then it's a dog.

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.340
Right?

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:10.430
I'm neither a cat
owner nor a dog owner,

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:13.370
so I probably offended
all sets of you.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:15.620
But the essential
thing is that you are

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.810
kind of building these rules.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:21.400
And those rules are
phenomenal, except they

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:23.570
really, really suck, right?

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:25.700
You cannot build those rules.

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:30.170
So in contrast, neural networks
are like this brain of ours

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:33.280
that some magic
happens, and we are

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:36.730
able to recognize
that that's a chair.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:41.310
And we're able to recognize
who's a man and who's a woman.

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:43.800
How we're doing
that, we don't know.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:46.510
So in some ways,
it is profoundly

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:51.080
dissatisfying,
profoundly dissatisfying

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:55.370
that this new branch
of machine intelligence

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:58.070
is not able to
explain to us how we

00:11:58.070 --> 00:11:59.930
are reaching those conclusions.

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:02.450
But it's also
profoundly satisfying

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:04.700
that it can actually
solve a set of problems

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:08.220
that you couldn't solve before.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:12.260
Neural networks have
come a long, long way.

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:16.040
They can recognize,
obviously, vision, speech,

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:18.410
complex constructs
that we have showed,

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:24.430
and languages and translations.

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:26.110
But that's all very
good, because what

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:28.830
neural networks says is that
you give it a set of data.

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:30.850
There are smart people
that say this is a cat

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:31.690
and this is a dog.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:35.342
And neural networks determine
what's really going on.

00:12:35.342 --> 00:12:36.300
What if you don't know?

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:40.660
What if you don't
have a training set?

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:41.530
What do you do?

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:46.890
So there's a new branch of
kind of machine intelligence

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:48.450
which is kind of--

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:50.190
one of those
branches is something

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.480
called reinforcement
learning, which

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:56.100
says that, throw the machine
in the wild and let it actually

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:58.260
figure out what's going on.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:00.630
And that is kind
of the concept that

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:06.490
was then used in the
next game that machines

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:09.770
beat human beings.

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:11.240
And that was Go.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:14.030
And if you play that
video, in the end,

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:16.970
just like Garry
Kasparov saying this,

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:21.280
just like Ken Jennings saying
we welcome our new overlords,

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:24.612
the Go master was kind of, whoa.

00:13:24.612 --> 00:13:25.320
What is going on?

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.890
Now, in this particular
case, I don't play Go,

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:31.980
but Go is infinitely
more complex than chess,

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:35.700
and simple deep computation
does not solve the problem.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:41.679
And Google's Deep Mind had to
bring reinforcement techniques,

00:13:41.679 --> 00:13:43.470
reinforcement learning
techniques, in order

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:46.320
to be able to beat
the Go champion.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:53.200
Now, quite recently,
machines have won on poker.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:55.120
Now, you would think
that poker is easy.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:57.625
For those of you
who are from India,

00:13:57.625 --> 00:13:59.430
we play a thing
called [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:02.470
I can't play poker, so I
don't know what poker is.

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:06.930
But I'm assured that
it is infinitely more

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:09.900
complex than any game that we
could have played in India.

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:17.290
So, but what a computer program
called Libratus from CMU

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:21.900
managed to beat
the poker champion.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:25.170
And it had three levels
of learning going on--

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:28.740
standard reinforcement,
standard programming,

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:30.690
reinforcement
learning based on what

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:34.120
it was observing during the day,
what the players were doing,

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:37.060
and then kind of
reprogramming at the night.

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:41.110
So human beings have
been able to program

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:47.390
the machines to beat other
human beings to play games.

00:14:47.390 --> 00:14:50.780
And there have been strong
advancement that's going on.

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:53.120
Right now, there
are no machines that

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:55.690
can program machines
to beat human beings.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:57.110
So it's safe for us.

00:14:57.110 --> 00:14:58.280
We are all there.

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:01.280
We actually help make all
of these programs happen.

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:03.380
But the advancements
are happening

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:05.616
based on these new
clusters of techniques that

00:15:05.616 --> 00:15:06.740
actually make a difference.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:12.330
Now, what that means
is that beyond games--

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:13.310
games are interesting.

00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:14.390
It's entertaining.

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:19.130
It's us versus them, and
we root for, depending

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.560
on which side we are, we
root for either machines

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:23.100
or human beings.

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:24.320
It's a phenomenal thing.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:28.580
But machine learning is
making a huge difference

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:30.250
in a lot of other areas.

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:31.680
Fei-Fei touched on it.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:36.684
In vision, machine learning
is making a big difference.

00:15:36.684 --> 00:15:38.100
Now, what's very,
very interesting

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:42.180
about vision is that it is
not just machine learning that

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:43.390
makes a difference.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:46.830
A lot of advancements in IT
happen when two or three forces

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.360
kind of come together.

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:51.900
And in this case, machine
learning combined with sensors

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:56.550
have actually helped really
advance a lot of this.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:59.010
Robots are becoming
much, much smarter.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:01.260
I don't know whether
any of you have ever

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:03.810
programmed in a programming
language called Forth.

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:06.360
Any raise of hands?

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:10.010
That's it, that distinguished
gentleman to my right.

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:14.960
And Forth was really designed
for helping kind of make moves.

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:17.630
But human beings were
completely in control.

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:21.060
And here, robots are kind
of learning themselves.

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:26.480
So the theme in all of this
is that the intelligence

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:32.360
has been used to power
either some interesting games

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:37.950
or some applications
that kind of bring

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:41.640
that intelligence to bear.

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:45.420
The next very, very important
trend that's happening,

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:48.210
and the trend that even
Fei-Fei talked about yesterday,

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:53.230
was this concept of
democratization of AI.

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:57.570
So AI was being built in the
context of either applications

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:02.160
that you didn't care about,
like games, or applications

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:05.819
that you cared about, like
vision and Street View.

00:17:05.819 --> 00:17:08.460
But AI was being
built in context

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:12.530
of all of those applications.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:15.589
What Google, as an example
here, has been doing

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:18.560
is to kind of
break that barrier,

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:24.290
break that barrier between
machine intelligence

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:28.880
and the applications, so that
now the applications actually

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:32.040
touch a lot more people.

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:36.510
And the common term that's
kind of being brought about

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:41.340
is this concept of
democratization of AI.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:46.440
And this is the first place
where APIs make a difference.

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:50.280
The breaking of the barrier
between an application

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:58.460
and a capability below
it happens through APIs.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:17:58.970
OK, great.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:00.680
So that's what's
really happening.

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:06.010
So APIs are actually enabling
these specialized algorithms

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:08.770
to get embedded in a
lot more applications.

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:13.930
And this is where, now, when
you get to the industrial age

00:18:13.930 --> 00:18:16.060
of AI, the impact will happen.

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:19.060
So it is not just the
intelligence and algorithms

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:22.360
built bespoke, but
intelligence and algorithms

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:25.250
applied across the board
that will make a difference.

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:28.000
So to give you some
examples, Google Photos

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:30.790
is just absolutely fantastic.

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:33.340
I have moved all my
stuff to Google Photos.

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:36.520
I don't have a dog out
there, but we typically,

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:38.560
when we are winding
down, Apigee,

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:41.920
we wanted to create a
workbook-- sorry, a photo book,

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:44.980
and I was able to kind of
search through the photos based

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:46.540
on finding people.

00:18:49.441 --> 00:18:51.815
Translation, machine translation
is a very tough problem.

00:18:51.815 --> 00:18:53.680
Some of you might
have seen the work

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:55.870
that Jeff Dean and
others did in order

00:18:55.870 --> 00:19:00.730
to get machine translation
built on neural networks.

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:06.370
And it has just been astounding
how much progress machine

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:07.980
translation has built.

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:11.380
But how many of us
really care about reading

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:14.990
that original stuff
in Italian or Latin

00:19:14.990 --> 00:19:17.800
and translating it into English?

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:21.010
But if you now think
about the application

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:24.400
of you are traveling and
you come across street signs

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:26.680
and you come across
subway signs,

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:29.530
how do you actually ensure that
the application that you're

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:32.630
dealing with has been
powered with intelligence?

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:37.180
So the break from
bespoke applications

00:19:37.180 --> 00:19:41.590
to taking intelligence
and applying it everywhere

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:44.980
is happening through APIs.

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:46.480
Prabhakar talked about it.

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:48.760
He had kind of raised
the bar on the numbers.

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:50.410
He said one in eight.

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:52.000
I thought it was one in 10.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:55.960
But for those people
who interact with

00:19:55.960 --> 00:20:01.664
me know that at least 10% of
responses that they get from me

00:20:01.664 --> 00:20:02.580
are machine-generated.

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:05.470
Now, the great thing is
they have no clue which one.

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:07.750
They have no clue which one.

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:13.210
But it's absolutely
mind-boggling

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:14.610
how good machine intelligence.

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:17.830
So there's another example of
how machine intelligence has

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:20.170
gotten democratized,
because now it is touching

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:24.100
people, everyday applications.

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:25.810
Search-- all of you use Search.

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:30.570
Search has phenomenal sets
of inputs associated with it.

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:34.050
When Larry and Sergey
built out Google,

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:36.900
they said that we are going to
build Search based on PageRank

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:41.490
so that it's not as important
as to how you say you are good.

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:44.970
It's how important how
others say you are good.

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:46.710
And PageRank then
turned out to be one

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:48.750
of the fundamental disruptors.

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:52.240
But over time, of course,
that is not the only signal.

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:55.200
And it turns out that
applying machine intelligence

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:58.630
and understanding user behavior
and others have really,

00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:04.200
really helped improve
the search technology.

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:06.260
So the net of all
of it is that we

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:11.610
are reaching the era of not
only advancements in AI,

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:17.220
but taking those AI through APIs
and applying it to applications

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:21.320
that we all look,
touch, and feel.

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:23.420
And in that context,
in that context,

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:26.510
as for those of you who
work in enterprises,

00:21:26.510 --> 00:21:27.950
think about the
problems that you

00:21:27.950 --> 00:21:32.850
have today that would be better
solved if you could actually

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:37.890
take some amount of machine
intelligence and power

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:39.140
these classes of applications.

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:42.050
Right, so I'm not going to
walk you through all of it.

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:45.170
All of you have ideas
and thought processes

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:46.730
on what can be done.

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:49.850
But things that
you couldn't do can

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:52.520
be done because of
advancements in AI

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:55.550
and the ability to couple
that AI into the application

00:21:55.550 --> 00:21:56.270
through APIs.

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:03.570
So the third part that I want
to just kind of talk about

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:04.500
is, this is great.

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:06.600
This is great.

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:11.720
Google and IBM and others
have done some innovation.

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:15.161
What's that got to
do with all of you?

00:22:15.161 --> 00:22:17.820
So the third major thing
that I want to talk about

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:19.620
is that what's
really going on is

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:23.870
that one aspect or
democratization of AI

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:27.620
is that the AI
techniques are more

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:29.970
easily available for everyone.

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:31.670
So what Google has
done, for example,

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:36.720
it has put out this
thing called TensorFlow--

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.680
those of you who want to
kind of geekify on tensors,

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:42.060
we can have a
discussion afterwards--

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:46.190
has put out this thing called
TensorFlow so that all of you

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:49.190
can actually
leverage your smarts

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:55.110
against some infrastructure
that Google provides.

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:57.890
But in addition,
Google has started

00:22:57.890 --> 00:23:00.590
to provide not just these
kind of raw building block

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:03.950
capabilities, which are shown
on the right, where you can

00:23:03.950 --> 00:23:06.770
actually take those raw building
block capabilities and apply

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:09.410
your talent and do
the right things.

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:12.830
But they're starting to package
these kind of building blocks

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:15.351
with an API in front of
them so that you don't have

00:23:15.351 --> 00:23:16.600
to worry about doing anything.

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:21.430
So on the left, I've shown kind
of these four building blocks.

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:23.590
Translation, if you really
care about translation,

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:25.440
send it a piece of
text through an API

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:27.830
and it will come
back translated.

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:29.209
Vision, send it an image.

00:23:29.209 --> 00:23:30.250
Now, just think about it.

00:23:30.250 --> 00:23:32.110
If you can send it
an image, you may

00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:33.850
be able to actually
figure out what

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:36.850
the damage is for a
particular property,

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:39.690
if you are in
property insurance.

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:46.240
Speech, the major trend towards
creating interfaces in speech

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:49.010
are just text understanding.

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:53.850
So what's really happening is
that this AI, which started off

00:23:53.850 --> 00:23:57.190
being driven by these
applications which

00:23:57.190 --> 00:24:01.410
were somewhat esoteric, like
playing games, to applications

00:24:01.410 --> 00:24:05.850
that required very tight
connection with AI,

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:07.590
those techniques
are now starting

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:11.310
to come out and be
available for all of you

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:14.342
in order to actually
make a difference.

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:22.090
So I've already kind of
touched on AI and APIs,

00:24:22.090 --> 00:24:24.820
but I want to just
kind of give you

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:33.240
somewhat further discussions
on kind of this duality.

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:36.750
So what we have
talked about is how

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:39.460
AI will be deeply
affected by APIs,

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:44.760
which is AI gets unleashed
for the masses through APIs.

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:54.000
Obviously, as I said,
whether you take some machine

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.790
intelligence, whether you
write any of your machine

00:24:56.790 --> 00:25:00.290
intelligence, but if
you really, really want

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:04.250
to power the myriads
of applications,

00:25:04.250 --> 00:25:07.160
then you need that gearbox.

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:09.140
That's our gearbox for APIs.

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:13.190
You need that gearbox that will
take all of that intelligence

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:15.770
and power your applications.

00:25:15.770 --> 00:25:21.830
Apigee has always been in the
business of decoupling services

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:23.360
with applications,
because you want

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:27.900
to make sure that the
innovation in the applications

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:30.750
happens at a rate that is
different than the innovation

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:33.660
in the services below it.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:36.690
The innovation in AI
happened by coupling

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:39.510
of the applications and AI.

00:25:39.510 --> 00:25:41.940
And now with APIs, this
barrier is breaking.

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:44.220
But there's a second very,
very fundamental thing

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:49.780
that's happening, is that
APIs are actually also

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:52.900
the way in which
data are flowing back

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:57.160
to make these machine
learning intelligence smarter.

00:25:57.160 --> 00:26:02.090
So there's duality
between intelligence,

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:06.140
APIs to power applications
based on their intelligence,

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:09.800
and then APIs to deliver data
back for those intelligence,

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:16.610
is that virtuous cycle that we
actually see a lot happening.

00:26:16.610 --> 00:26:18.240
But that duality is also true.

00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:24.750
AI will deeply affect APIs.

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:28.440
So for those of you
who are writing APIs,

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:31.110
for those of you who
are writing APIs,

00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:36.850
you need to think through
how AI will deeply affect

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:38.430
the APIs that you're writing.

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:40.500
So I'm going to
spend a few minutes

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:46.590
in kind of getting you to
think about your world of APIs

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:50.553
and to see how AI will actually
make a difference there.

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:56.360
So in order to do
that, I'm going

00:26:56.360 --> 00:27:00.680
to actually blow past some
assumption that you have.

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:09.280
And what I want to do is
to get you a bit anxious

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:12.990
and think through and say, oh
my god, if the world is really

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:17.734
different, how I've
been thinking of APIs,

00:27:17.734 --> 00:27:19.400
how will I think about
them differently?

00:27:19.400 --> 00:27:26.340
So as a first example, I've
shown an industrial economy

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:27.880
and a smokestack.

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:34.700
So pardon me, but typically,
many of us, many of you

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:39.107
think of end consumers
as your customers.

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:46.250
What if you're wrong?

00:27:46.250 --> 00:27:52.580
What if instead of you being
at the center of the universe

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:57.550
where people come to you
and get your services,

00:27:57.550 --> 00:28:00.040
the thing is completely flipped?

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:05.690
What if, what if the consumer is
at the center of the universe,

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:12.040
and the consumer chooses, she
chooses to interact with you?

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:14.350
In that case, when
you're thinking

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:21.070
about your world of
interacting with that consumer,

00:28:21.070 --> 00:28:25.020
how would you embed intelligence
into every interaction?

00:28:25.020 --> 00:28:31.244
Because the person
chooses to come to you.

00:28:31.244 --> 00:28:33.440
You don't choose them.

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:34.557
If that's the case--

00:28:34.557 --> 00:28:36.140
and there's a
phenomenal piece of work

00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:40.160
by a person called Nir Eyal
called "The Hook Framework"--

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:43.190
how would you actually
use intelligence

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:47.390
to hook that person, so that
among all the time that she

00:28:47.390 --> 00:28:50.250
would spend with
you, she actually

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:53.760
gets most fun and most
interactions with you?

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:55.050
The world is not slowing down.

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:56.190
It is kind of slowing down.

00:28:56.190 --> 00:28:57.540
I know that's true.

00:28:57.540 --> 00:28:58.530
It's actually true.

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:00.900
We'll probably
add about a minute

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:04.230
to Earth's rotation in
the next million years.

00:29:04.230 --> 00:29:06.830
But that's not sufficient to
actually create more time.

00:29:06.830 --> 00:29:09.870
The world is not slowing
down, then how would

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:13.266
you actually hook the people
in order to actually do that?

00:29:13.266 --> 00:29:14.640
And that's where
you got to think

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:19.410
about embedding AI into
every piece of interaction

00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:20.130
that you do.

00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:22.680
And if you believe that you do
the interaction through apps

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:25.610
that are connected
by APIs, you actually

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:29.040
have to embed AI into
every piece of interaction,

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:31.480
into every API.

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:33.845
What if you kept
thinking that AI

00:29:33.845 --> 00:29:35.780
is for esoteric applications?

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:38.590
What if you thought
that AI is for games?

00:29:38.590 --> 00:29:43.300
What if you thought that
AI is for diagnostics?

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:44.980
All great things.

00:29:44.980 --> 00:29:45.790
All great things.

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:48.880
But what if you thought that
AI is actually not going

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:53.480
to affect you in any deep way?

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:56.650
But in reality, if it is the
case that every application

00:29:56.650 --> 00:29:58.540
needs to become a
learning application,

00:29:58.540 --> 00:30:03.490
if every application needs
to actually adapt and learn,

00:30:03.490 --> 00:30:04.930
what if that's the case?

00:30:04.930 --> 00:30:07.420
The examples that I showed you.

00:30:07.420 --> 00:30:10.240
Then you have to
embed intelligence

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:14.500
into every
application, every API.

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:15.670
So think about it.

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:19.390
As you think about
building your API layer,

00:30:19.390 --> 00:30:22.390
building a new class of
applications, if you're not

00:30:22.390 --> 00:30:25.360
going to actually embed
intelligence in that layer,

00:30:25.360 --> 00:30:29.472
then you're going to
actually be left behind.

00:30:29.472 --> 00:30:31.180
The third thing that
I want to talk about

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:33.849
is what if you thought that
your network ended on your four

00:30:33.849 --> 00:30:34.348
walls?

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:40.660
That's what we typically
do, for those of us who

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:43.520
are in the enterprise world.

00:30:43.520 --> 00:30:46.740
But in reality, what
if the network actually

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:50.470
extended everywhere?

00:30:50.470 --> 00:30:53.570
There are no four walls.

00:30:53.570 --> 00:30:56.570
The touch points,
the interactions,

00:30:56.570 --> 00:30:58.700
all have moved to the edge.

00:31:02.470 --> 00:31:07.180
Now you have to
embed intelligence

00:31:07.180 --> 00:31:10.990
into every piece of the network.

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:13.900
The way to embed the
intelligence, of course,

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:17.440
is by taking your enterprise
capabilities through APIs.

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:20.110
But those, you have
got to actually power

00:31:20.110 --> 00:31:23.320
and embed with AI.

00:31:23.320 --> 00:31:30.710
You will embed intelligence
into your edge applications

00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:35.020
for better operations, for
better customer interactions,

00:31:35.020 --> 00:31:36.350
and for security.

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:38.870
So what I've tried to do over
these kind of three things

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:43.600
is to say that as you have
embarked upon your API journey

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:46.010
to create this kind
of digital world

00:31:46.010 --> 00:31:49.060
in which many more
forms of interaction

00:31:49.060 --> 00:31:53.480
is going to happen with you,
if you ignore the world of AI,

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.090
you're actually
going to be building

00:31:55.090 --> 00:31:57.460
to the world of
yesterday, as opposed

00:31:57.460 --> 00:31:58.510
to the world of tomorrow.

00:31:58.510 --> 00:32:02.230
So instead of thinking
about, oh, the best I can do

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:04.870
is to actually just
connect my mobile app

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:07.420
to my back-end system, you
really have to think about,

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:11.390
how do you make that connection
to be smart, intelligent,

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:14.330
and secure?

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:16.480
So just as an example,
we provide you

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:19.120
with a product called
Apigee Edge that enables

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:22.007
you to build up those APIs.

00:32:22.007 --> 00:32:23.590
And one of the things
that we're doing

00:32:23.590 --> 00:32:26.290
is we are applying
machine intelligence so

00:32:26.290 --> 00:32:30.010
that as you expose your
APIs through Apigee,

00:32:30.010 --> 00:32:31.540
machine intelligence
can actually

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:35.410
help you detect when the
interactions that are coming in

00:32:35.410 --> 00:32:37.610
are legitimate, and
when they are not.

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:40.780
But you have got to think
through every interaction

00:32:40.780 --> 00:32:44.020
that's going on and seeing
how you can actually

00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:48.880
leverage that power for
better interactions and better

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:49.400
security.

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:55.580
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to call Jason Smith here,

00:32:55.580 --> 00:32:58.190
who has been working in
this area of AI and API

00:32:58.190 --> 00:33:00.620
for a long time, and he's
going to kind of walk you

00:33:00.620 --> 00:33:02.780
through what they're
doing at rMark Bio.

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:05.170
And then I'll come
back and conclude.

00:33:05.170 --> 00:33:07.153
Welcome, Jason.

00:33:07.153 --> 00:33:11.269
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:11.269 --> 00:33:12.227
JASON SMITH: Thank you.

00:33:12.227 --> 00:33:13.450
I'm taking it off.

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:16.210
So I'm the co-founder
and CEO of rMark Bio,

00:33:16.210 --> 00:33:19.180
and what we've set out to do
with artificial intelligence

00:33:19.180 --> 00:33:23.980
is to leverage AI to help reduce
the inefficiencies in which

00:33:23.980 --> 00:33:26.000
life science
companies-- for example,

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:28.120
pharmaceutical
companies-- can partner

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:32.020
with various researchers,
clinical partners,

00:33:32.020 --> 00:33:34.690
and academic institutions
based on the various stages

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:36.550
of their drugs going
through development,

00:33:36.550 --> 00:33:40.180
and ultimately, anyone in
the health care market.

00:33:40.180 --> 00:33:42.490
So where we were at, we've
designed the blueprint

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:45.270
for really transforming
this global health care data

00:33:45.270 --> 00:33:46.750
into business intelligence.

00:33:46.750 --> 00:33:48.640
And this is really
important for what

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:50.806
we want to solve that's
different than what everyone

00:33:50.806 --> 00:33:53.380
else has tried to do before,
because today, it's all

00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:55.900
been about having
data scientists attack

00:33:55.900 --> 00:33:58.914
the problem who do not speak
the language of the business,

00:33:58.914 --> 00:34:00.580
or you have business
strategists saying,

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:04.060
we need these type of people,
and the data scientists don't

00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:05.950
understand what
that fundamentally

00:34:05.950 --> 00:34:08.259
means to go extract that
from all the global health

00:34:08.259 --> 00:34:11.190
care data out there.

00:34:11.190 --> 00:34:13.204
So the first thing
we knew we had

00:34:13.204 --> 00:34:15.620
to do when we started this
project about two years ago was

00:34:15.620 --> 00:34:17.210
we had to break
down the barriers.

00:34:17.210 --> 00:34:19.400
We could build the coolest
platform in the world,

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:21.860
but if we couldn't
consistently get data in there,

00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:23.100
it meant nothing.

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:25.730
Then, when we go
to our customers,

00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:28.560
if we couldn't get their
data into our platform,

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:31.550
it meant nothing, because the
unique value that we wanted

00:34:31.550 --> 00:34:35.600
to solve was that ability
to translate between data

00:34:35.600 --> 00:34:38.179
and the business
needs in real time.

00:34:38.179 --> 00:34:39.710
So our approach
that we've started

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:43.310
to go after was, how
do we create a platform

00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:46.429
and then create APIs around each
one of these tiny little bits

00:34:46.429 --> 00:34:48.110
so we can slowly,
over the few years,

00:34:48.110 --> 00:34:50.900
build up a very large
platform of microservices

00:34:50.900 --> 00:34:52.520
that we can leverage
independently

00:34:52.520 --> 00:34:54.977
of each other based on
different customer needs?

00:34:54.977 --> 00:34:56.810
So I think the best way
to walk through this

00:34:56.810 --> 00:34:59.240
is I'll actually walk you
guys through an example

00:34:59.240 --> 00:35:02.030
of a customer problem that
we've recently solved.

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:04.010
So a customer, a large
pharmaceutical company

00:35:04.010 --> 00:35:06.860
came to us and said, look,
we've got all this clinical data

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:07.640
internally.

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:10.760
We have all this ongoing
R&amp;D data going internally.

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:14.200
We also have all the global
health data you guys have.

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:15.890
What we don't
understand how to do

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:18.732
is to align that in
real time with who

00:35:18.732 --> 00:35:19.940
we should be partnering with.

00:35:19.940 --> 00:35:23.400
So we want to go to understand
that if we're going to roll out

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:25.330
a leukemia drug, or
we're going to roll out

00:35:25.330 --> 00:35:27.250
a multiple sclerosis
drug, there's

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:29.990
certain places in the world
where these patients are highly

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:30.509
populated.

00:35:30.509 --> 00:35:33.050
There's certain places in the
world where their competition's

00:35:33.050 --> 00:35:33.929
already there.

00:35:33.929 --> 00:35:35.720
And there's certain
places where their drug

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:39.080
is going to have higher
efficacy treating certain types

00:35:39.080 --> 00:35:41.120
of patients than not.

00:35:41.120 --> 00:35:43.700
They came to us to say,
help us understand how to,

00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:45.440
A, pull all this data in.

00:35:45.440 --> 00:35:47.756
Help us to understand
what this data really

00:35:47.756 --> 00:35:49.880
means so we can go out and
create deeper engagement

00:35:49.880 --> 00:35:51.020
strategies.

00:35:51.020 --> 00:35:52.970
And then third, how
do we learn from it?

00:35:52.970 --> 00:35:54.380
How do we get feedback?

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:58.700
Having rMark go in and survey
their people in the field,

00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:00.380
saying, was that a
good recommendation?

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:02.004
Did you like the
person we recommended?

00:36:02.004 --> 00:36:02.769
Did that align?

00:36:02.769 --> 00:36:03.810
That's not going to work.

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:06.920
So we knew right away we had
to build in some mechanism

00:36:06.920 --> 00:36:10.100
to receive data in a learning
model and learning information

00:36:10.100 --> 00:36:11.957
back from our
customers in real time

00:36:11.957 --> 00:36:13.290
who's actually out in the field.

00:36:16.090 --> 00:36:18.167
So when we started
to engage in this,

00:36:18.167 --> 00:36:19.750
we have our global
health data that we

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:22.660
pull in from every night
from all over the world.

00:36:22.660 --> 00:36:25.360
We quickly realized that these
pharmaceutical companies,

00:36:25.360 --> 00:36:27.220
despite being inside
the four walls,

00:36:27.220 --> 00:36:29.050
actually have the exact
same problem, where

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:30.790
their data is highly siloed.

00:36:30.790 --> 00:36:32.510
So you have omics data.

00:36:32.510 --> 00:36:37.450
So this is genetics,
genomics, proteomics,

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:40.910
people who are actually running
the real science out there

00:36:40.910 --> 00:36:42.557
at the fundamental target level.

00:36:42.557 --> 00:36:44.890
You actually have the internal
drug data, all their data

00:36:44.890 --> 00:36:47.290
through the FDA process,
what they went through,

00:36:47.290 --> 00:36:49.660
the reports they cited,
everything of that nature.

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:52.507
They have practitioner
databases, what's

00:36:52.507 --> 00:36:53.590
happening in the industry.

00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:55.960
I have databases
from IMS and others

00:36:55.960 --> 00:36:58.030
about what practitioners
are actually doing

00:36:58.030 --> 00:37:00.490
and who they're targeting
and who they're helping.

00:37:00.490 --> 00:37:02.110
But that sits
completely isolated.

00:37:02.110 --> 00:37:04.780
Again, insurance data, and
then they have research data.

00:37:04.780 --> 00:37:08.140
They have teams of people
going out there and pulling

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:10.745
in all this global health care
data, but it's sitting siloed,

00:37:10.745 --> 00:37:12.370
and any of the machine
learning and any

00:37:12.370 --> 00:37:15.590
of the analytics on this is
completely separate from all

00:37:15.590 --> 00:37:18.790
these other groups.

00:37:18.790 --> 00:37:20.760
So in our conversations,
we quickly

00:37:20.760 --> 00:37:24.240
found that data was really
problematic for them.

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:26.190
First, they have the three V's.

00:37:26.190 --> 00:37:30.990
They just cannot handle the
size and the change in which

00:37:30.990 --> 00:37:32.730
the data comes in.

00:37:32.730 --> 00:37:35.820
Then, based on those silos,
how do they even exchange it?

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:36.697
How do they share it?

00:37:36.697 --> 00:37:38.030
What do they want to do with it?

00:37:38.030 --> 00:37:40.870
They're not even sure
what questions to ask.

00:37:40.870 --> 00:37:43.780
So they had no really idea how
to draw insights across all

00:37:43.780 --> 00:37:45.450
these different data silos.

00:37:45.450 --> 00:37:47.760
And then once you have
that, how do you translate

00:37:47.760 --> 00:37:52.890
that in real time to, I need a
team to go to Washington State

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:54.770
and find these
five practitioners

00:37:54.770 --> 00:38:00.110
and engage with them, because
we need their clinical studies?

00:38:00.110 --> 00:38:02.240
And then most importantly,
AI was problematic.

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:06.320
We found great data
scientists internally,

00:38:06.320 --> 00:38:09.180
but were really isolated
to their specialty.

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:10.460
So they understood omics.

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:14.930
They understood how to
parse a grant paper.

00:38:14.930 --> 00:38:19.589
They understood how to apply NLP
to a PubMed document on scale.

00:38:19.589 --> 00:38:21.380
But they didn't quite
know how to translate

00:38:21.380 --> 00:38:25.250
that skill set to other
data sets and other teams

00:38:25.250 --> 00:38:26.469
within the organization.

00:38:26.469 --> 00:38:28.010
And fundamentally,
they were spending

00:38:28.010 --> 00:38:31.430
a lot of time and money doing
very large-scale partnerships

00:38:31.430 --> 00:38:34.880
to try to solve this problem.

00:38:34.880 --> 00:38:37.940
And there were, at the end
the day, missed consequences.

00:38:37.940 --> 00:38:40.615
When you have trouble with the
data, you have trouble with AI,

00:38:40.615 --> 00:38:41.990
we're having missed
consequences.

00:38:41.990 --> 00:38:43.190
They're missing opportunities.

00:38:43.190 --> 00:38:44.940
They're spending a
lot of time and money,

00:38:44.940 --> 00:38:46.400
and most importantly,
by the time

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:49.985
they get done with one
subset of analysis,

00:38:49.985 --> 00:38:51.860
they had to go back and
do it all over again.

00:38:54.460 --> 00:38:58.320
So our approach was, OK, we can
apply artificial intelligence

00:38:58.320 --> 00:38:59.400
to solve a lot of this.

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:01.830
And Anant took us on
a nice game journey.

00:39:01.830 --> 00:39:03.432
This is going to
be a little drier.

00:39:03.432 --> 00:39:04.890
This is going to
be how we actually

00:39:04.890 --> 00:39:06.480
took the approach
with them, and how

00:39:06.480 --> 00:39:09.360
we leveraged some of
the Apigee systems

00:39:09.360 --> 00:39:12.070
and then ultimately some of the
Google technologies for this.

00:39:12.070 --> 00:39:14.490
So the first thing we
knew we had to do on scale

00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:17.700
and build microservices around
and created an API that we

00:39:17.700 --> 00:39:20.310
could reuse every single
time to get this data

00:39:20.310 --> 00:39:23.190
was to ingest all this
global health care data

00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:25.470
every single night,
normalize it, and prep it

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:27.175
for future analysis.

00:39:27.175 --> 00:39:29.550
Then, once we had that, we
had to start building learning

00:39:29.550 --> 00:39:30.059
models.

00:39:30.059 --> 00:39:31.850
We will talk about this
a little bit later,

00:39:31.850 --> 00:39:33.016
but we leveraged TensorFlow.

00:39:33.016 --> 00:39:35.310
We leveraged some internal
algorithms that we developed

00:39:35.310 --> 00:39:36.750
for our deep learning platform.

00:39:36.750 --> 00:39:39.180
We have to start taking
this data in and letting

00:39:39.180 --> 00:39:41.970
the system understand
what it is and learn.

00:39:41.970 --> 00:39:43.554
Next, we had to get
our customer data.

00:39:43.554 --> 00:39:45.303
Now, this is where the
real challenge was.

00:39:45.303 --> 00:39:47.970
As I discussed, there's multiple
silos across multiple business

00:39:47.970 --> 00:39:50.350
and technology divisions.

00:39:50.350 --> 00:39:52.890
There's also just silos
within the technology.

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:55.725
Very often, when you go into
very large scale enterprises

00:39:55.725 --> 00:39:57.960
that have been
around for 80 years,

00:39:57.960 --> 00:39:59.810
you're going to have
data systems that

00:39:59.810 --> 00:40:01.030
aren't readily available.

00:40:01.030 --> 00:40:04.950
It's not all going to
be a node database,

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:08.640
or it's not going to be just
up on Datastore, Couchbase,

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:10.599
and JSON, which is
really nice and easy.

00:40:10.599 --> 00:40:12.390
It's going to be
something a little gnarly,

00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:14.290
that you got to figure
out, what do I actually

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:15.998
need to get out of
here and how do I even

00:40:15.998 --> 00:40:18.360
access the systems
behind IT firewalls

00:40:18.360 --> 00:40:20.400
in a highly-regulated system?

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:22.690
And then ultimately, as a
business, this is great.

00:40:22.690 --> 00:40:23.880
We can do this one time.

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:26.255
We have to figure out how to
create a platform that I can

00:40:26.255 --> 00:40:28.540
open up to not only ourselves
for our applications,

00:40:28.540 --> 00:40:30.167
but we can't build
every application

00:40:30.167 --> 00:40:31.500
in the world for every customer.

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:34.440
We want them eventually to
be empowered to take our APIs

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:37.140
and take our platform and
build their own applications,

00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:38.820
integrate it deeper
at different points

00:40:38.820 --> 00:40:40.990
within the tools they use.

00:40:40.990 --> 00:40:44.200
We're not the ones
always doing it.

00:40:44.200 --> 00:40:47.360
So the first one was
store and gather,

00:40:47.360 --> 00:40:48.790
or gather and store, actually.

00:40:48.790 --> 00:40:51.290
So for this, we went around.

00:40:51.290 --> 00:40:54.250
We created a series of
microservices using the Apigee

00:40:54.250 --> 00:40:57.790
platform for our
APIs using Edge that

00:40:57.790 --> 00:41:02.470
talked to these individual
behind-the-scenes data sources.

00:41:02.470 --> 00:41:04.840
So we were able to start
pulling in the data,

00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:07.360
ingesting it, understanding it.

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:09.580
For us, we used DataProc
just for the volume.

00:41:09.580 --> 00:41:12.160
I don't know how many of you
here have used that before.

00:41:12.160 --> 00:41:12.910
We love it.

00:41:12.910 --> 00:41:14.785
We're huge fans of it,
coming over from Spark

00:41:14.785 --> 00:41:17.680
and not having to manage
all that ourselves.

00:41:17.680 --> 00:41:19.347
And then we've recently
started engaging

00:41:19.347 --> 00:41:20.763
with Cloud Functions,
where that's

00:41:20.763 --> 00:41:23.020
been helping a lot with just
the onset scale of data,

00:41:23.020 --> 00:41:25.630
because customers are
constantly updating.

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:26.950
They have data that's changing.

00:41:26.950 --> 00:41:29.170
They're running hundreds
and thousands of experiments

00:41:29.170 --> 00:41:30.230
a month at times.

00:41:30.230 --> 00:41:32.590
They have people in the
constantly field learning

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:34.330
new things, and
we have to ingest

00:41:34.330 --> 00:41:38.080
all that in real time, plus
all the global health data.

00:41:38.080 --> 00:41:38.950
And then storage.

00:41:38.950 --> 00:41:40.612
Where do we store this data?

00:41:40.612 --> 00:41:42.820
For this particular project,
we leveraged Cloud Store

00:41:42.820 --> 00:41:45.910
and Data Store, because
of the nature for the data

00:41:45.910 --> 00:41:47.680
and that it made the
most sense for how

00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:51.290
we were going to post-process
it for our platform.

00:41:51.290 --> 00:41:53.650
So then next after this,
we have the data in there.

00:41:53.650 --> 00:41:55.358
We start looking at
where are the models.

00:41:55.358 --> 00:41:57.310
So we're big fans of TensorFlow.

00:41:57.310 --> 00:42:00.550
A lot of our core
matrices algorithms run,

00:42:00.550 --> 00:42:02.416
so TensorFlow has
a nice alignment

00:42:02.416 --> 00:42:03.790
with where we were
kind of headed

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:07.780
with some SVD and other
techniques we're leveraging.

00:42:07.780 --> 00:42:10.030
And then just some of the
natural language processing.

00:42:10.030 --> 00:42:14.650
We do still have to pull out key
concepts, key words, sentiment.

00:42:14.650 --> 00:42:18.154
We know from pulling in from
Salesforce data in real time,

00:42:18.154 --> 00:42:20.320
if someone is out in the
field and spoke to a doctor

00:42:20.320 --> 00:42:22.450
and they hear about these
new patients they have,

00:42:22.450 --> 00:42:24.460
that have the rare
disease, we can

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:27.190
ingest that in real time,
flag that, and push that out,

00:42:27.190 --> 00:42:30.160
so someone in the business team
can make a real-time decision

00:42:30.160 --> 00:42:33.380
off of that information.

00:42:33.380 --> 00:42:35.250
And then how do we put
services around it?

00:42:35.250 --> 00:42:38.540
So if I walked into my customers
and said, here's all the data,

00:42:38.540 --> 00:42:42.740
and here's 42 different
microservices and 50 APIs,

00:42:42.740 --> 00:42:43.970
that's not going to solve it.

00:42:43.970 --> 00:42:46.760
We had to bundle this
up into API packages

00:42:46.760 --> 00:42:49.490
that they could use
and they could access

00:42:49.490 --> 00:42:51.699
and they felt were
secure, most importantly,

00:42:51.699 --> 00:42:53.240
because at the end
of all this, we're

00:42:53.240 --> 00:42:56.960
in a highly-regulated industry
that requires, if nothing else,

00:42:56.960 --> 00:42:59.370
discretion and security
first and foremost.

00:42:59.370 --> 00:43:02.130
So we built three primary
services for our customer

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:04.500
on this particular project,
on top of our platform.

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:06.680
One was the real-time
recommendations.

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:10.100
So this is our
learning process that

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:13.800
understands who to recommend
to the people in the field.

00:43:13.800 --> 00:43:17.300
So for example, if you're a
top-10 pharmaceutical company,

00:43:17.300 --> 00:43:21.050
you have anywhere between
500 and 2,000 people

00:43:21.050 --> 00:43:23.420
you employ that go out
all around the world.

00:43:23.420 --> 00:43:25.700
Their entire job
is to find people

00:43:25.700 --> 00:43:29.360
to partner with, either doctors,
either academic researchers,

00:43:29.360 --> 00:43:31.850
maybe more strategic as
an entire institution.

00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:34.070
This is all they do.

00:43:34.070 --> 00:43:36.680
And it's a very manual
labor intensive.

00:43:36.680 --> 00:43:40.460
This service bifurcates
that manual process for them

00:43:40.460 --> 00:43:42.680
to a large degree, where
we can give them real-time

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:45.140
recommendations about what's
happening in the field that

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:46.510
they're in--

00:43:46.510 --> 00:43:48.980
a therapeutic area, in
a regional location--

00:43:48.980 --> 00:43:53.140
that aligns with their
global business strategies.

00:43:53.140 --> 00:43:54.170
Learning and feedback.

00:43:54.170 --> 00:43:59.630
So Anant made a great point,
and we couldn't agree more.

00:43:59.630 --> 00:44:01.820
None of this matters if
it's all push, if you don't

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:04.970
start pulling, and letting
the data reinforce and train

00:44:04.970 --> 00:44:09.307
our models for us, not from
instant thumbs up, thumbs down.

00:44:09.307 --> 00:44:10.640
It's got to be deeper than that.

00:44:10.640 --> 00:44:15.260
So we need to look at everything
from when we integrate

00:44:15.260 --> 00:44:17.120
with Salesforce, we
integrate with Viva,

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:20.390
we integrate with a
myriad of applications.

00:44:20.390 --> 00:44:23.640
Our customers, users,
touch every single day,

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:26.070
and their employees
touch every single day.

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:28.970
We need to be able to bring
all that in, and automatically

00:44:28.970 --> 00:44:31.640
continue to train
our models such

00:44:31.640 --> 00:44:35.040
that we can leverage this by
the real-time recommendations

00:44:35.040 --> 00:44:38.180
and by the clinical insight
service, which normally we'd

00:44:38.180 --> 00:44:39.350
never talk to each other.

00:44:39.350 --> 00:44:41.180
Those services never
talk to each other.

00:44:41.180 --> 00:44:44.870
But because we're getting that
constant feedback from them,

00:44:44.870 --> 00:44:47.969
we're giving that constant,
hey, I like this person.

00:44:47.969 --> 00:44:49.010
I don't like this person.

00:44:49.010 --> 00:44:50.330
But we're not asking them that.

00:44:50.330 --> 00:44:52.010
We're able to interpret that.

00:44:52.010 --> 00:44:53.840
We can provide that
through our APIs

00:44:53.840 --> 00:44:55.460
back to the real-time
recommendations,

00:44:55.460 --> 00:44:58.880
back to our clinical insights
model, through the APIs.

00:44:58.880 --> 00:45:01.820
And now we're training
three models in parallel

00:45:01.820 --> 00:45:05.000
through this platform.

00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:07.340
And then ultimately, Edge.

00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:08.870
Edge is the way we push it out.

00:45:08.870 --> 00:45:10.820
So as I mentioned
earlier, Edge provided us

00:45:10.820 --> 00:45:14.690
with the fundamental
infrastructure, the security,

00:45:14.690 --> 00:45:18.080
and the deployment stability
that we needed from Apigee

00:45:18.080 --> 00:45:23.330
to allow our customers to feel
safe, embed this API into some

00:45:23.330 --> 00:45:25.040
of their existing applications.

00:45:25.040 --> 00:45:26.540
In this particular
case, we actually

00:45:26.540 --> 00:45:30.030
built a one-off custom app
for them on top of our APIs.

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:32.690
They embedded it in Salesforce
and another proprietary tool

00:45:32.690 --> 00:45:33.770
they had in-house.

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:37.130
So now, all through Edge, we
have our app using these APIs

00:45:37.130 --> 00:45:39.650
that their employees
are using, and all

00:45:39.650 --> 00:45:41.480
these other third-party
applications

00:45:41.480 --> 00:45:45.890
are now accessing it
through the Edge platform.

00:45:45.890 --> 00:45:51.470
So essentially, we set out to
solve a fairly large problem

00:45:51.470 --> 00:45:52.300
in this space.

00:45:52.300 --> 00:45:55.310
And we think we are.

00:45:55.310 --> 00:45:57.690
We think we're headed
in the right direction.

00:45:57.690 --> 00:46:01.940
And ultimately, it comes down
to putting lots of intelligence

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:05.280
behind every little piece
along the way of the pipeline

00:46:05.280 --> 00:46:06.925
and exposing that
through APIs such

00:46:06.925 --> 00:46:09.050
that we don't have to solve
all the problems today.

00:46:09.050 --> 00:46:12.250
I don't have to have every
data source integrated today.

00:46:12.250 --> 00:46:15.390
I don't have to have every
learning model figured out

00:46:15.390 --> 00:46:15.890
today.

00:46:15.890 --> 00:46:18.306
I don't have to have every
need of my customer figured out

00:46:18.306 --> 00:46:18.890
today.

00:46:18.890 --> 00:46:20.660
I can stitch and
build these as I go.

00:46:20.660 --> 00:46:22.160
I can modify my models.

00:46:22.160 --> 00:46:24.110
I can modify my ingest.

00:46:24.110 --> 00:46:26.750
And done correctly, I
don't have to modify

00:46:26.750 --> 00:46:27.770
a lot of my interfaces.

00:46:27.770 --> 00:46:31.710
And when I do, I've got Edge
to help me migrate my customers

00:46:31.710 --> 00:46:32.900
from my internal platform.

00:46:32.900 --> 00:46:35.000
So we don't just use
Edge for our customers.

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:37.516
We actually use this for all
of our internal microservices

00:46:37.516 --> 00:46:39.140
all the way through
the platform, which

00:46:39.140 --> 00:46:41.014
now is somewhere in the
neighborhood of about

00:46:41.014 --> 00:46:44.360
25 different microservices
that we just consume internally

00:46:44.360 --> 00:46:48.350
long before we even
get to our customer.

00:46:48.350 --> 00:46:49.090
Thank you.

00:46:49.090 --> 00:46:50.006
ANANT JHINGRAN: Great.

00:46:50.006 --> 00:46:50.635
Thank you.

00:46:50.635 --> 00:46:54.820
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:54.820 --> 00:46:57.520
This thing is heavy if
it is not clipped in.

00:46:57.520 --> 00:46:59.890
Thank you so much.

00:46:59.890 --> 00:47:00.750
OK.

00:47:00.750 --> 00:47:03.500
So what we have done
is we have kind of--

00:47:03.500 --> 00:47:07.780
I talked a bit about
this advancement of AI

00:47:07.780 --> 00:47:10.570
and how it's gone from
siloed applications

00:47:10.570 --> 00:47:13.390
to being democratized
through APIs.

00:47:13.390 --> 00:47:16.300
We talked about how, if you
are embarking on an API journey

00:47:16.300 --> 00:47:20.620
by itself, you got
to think about AI

00:47:20.620 --> 00:47:22.970
as powering all of
the interactions that

00:47:22.970 --> 00:47:25.630
are enabled by APIs.

00:47:25.630 --> 00:47:29.710
And then Jason talked through
a wonderful set of examples

00:47:29.710 --> 00:47:37.660
of how, in the area that he's
in, APIs enable better AI,

00:47:37.660 --> 00:47:40.120
and then AI enables
better APIs, and there

00:47:40.120 --> 00:47:43.270
is this kind of feedback
loop that's going on.

00:47:43.270 --> 00:47:45.040
And for those of
you who are kind

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:50.920
of thinking through live
example of AI being applied,

00:47:50.920 --> 00:47:52.870
just look at the
two blue screens

00:47:52.870 --> 00:47:54.550
on the left and the right.

00:47:54.550 --> 00:47:57.370
And there's nobody sitting
out there transcribing.

00:47:57.370 --> 00:48:01.480
This is all going on by
speech-to-text translations

00:48:01.480 --> 00:48:03.490
and others that are going on.

00:48:03.490 --> 00:48:08.874
So this is the fundamental
point that we have

00:48:08.874 --> 00:48:10.040
tried to make in this thing.

00:48:10.040 --> 00:48:12.710
But I want to just
step back a bit

00:48:12.710 --> 00:48:14.960
and say that if you
really want to embark

00:48:14.960 --> 00:48:18.650
on an AI and an
API-driven journey,

00:48:18.650 --> 00:48:21.060
technology will be easy.

00:48:21.060 --> 00:48:23.090
The reason technology
is easy is because there

00:48:23.090 --> 00:48:25.730
are enough
advancements happening

00:48:25.730 --> 00:48:28.670
and they are being released
out in the wild for you

00:48:28.670 --> 00:48:31.100
to actually make use of.

00:48:31.100 --> 00:48:33.350
What you all have
to think through

00:48:33.350 --> 00:48:36.170
is the people aspect of it.

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:38.450
Other day I read
something which said,

00:48:38.450 --> 00:48:41.180
or every doctor will have
to become a data scientist.

00:48:41.180 --> 00:48:44.410
I said, what the heck
is going on here?

00:48:44.410 --> 00:48:45.380
Right?

00:48:45.380 --> 00:48:48.140
And then tons of people did
thumbs up, thumbs up on it.

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:50.030
Wow, sounded really,
really interesting.

00:48:50.030 --> 00:48:50.930
Every doctor.

00:48:50.930 --> 00:48:56.931
The doctor barely has
time to look after you.

00:48:56.931 --> 00:48:58.680
Do you think that she
has time to actually

00:48:58.680 --> 00:49:00.080
now become a data scientist?

00:49:00.080 --> 00:49:01.550
That's not going to happen.

00:49:01.550 --> 00:49:03.710
But what's really
going to happen

00:49:03.710 --> 00:49:07.220
is that you have to
think through how

00:49:07.220 --> 00:49:13.100
your developers start leveraging
the work of data scientists.

00:49:13.100 --> 00:49:15.170
You have got to think
about how you're

00:49:15.170 --> 00:49:19.460
going to disaggregate your
machine intelligence and AI

00:49:19.460 --> 00:49:23.296
organizations and scatter
them into the rest

00:49:23.296 --> 00:49:24.170
of your organization.

00:49:24.170 --> 00:49:28.370
Ken Rudin, who was
the CTO for Zynga

00:49:28.370 --> 00:49:30.890
before he went to
Facebook, Ken Rudin

00:49:30.890 --> 00:49:37.310
said that he actually took his
analytics and intelligence team

00:49:37.310 --> 00:49:39.830
and broke it apart
and embedded it

00:49:39.830 --> 00:49:44.390
into every team that
was building games,

00:49:44.390 --> 00:49:48.530
because it was easy for
that team to come together,

00:49:48.530 --> 00:49:51.500
but it was very difficult
for the developers

00:49:51.500 --> 00:49:53.930
to actively think about
data and intelligence.

00:49:53.930 --> 00:49:56.420
So as you think
about the role of AI,

00:49:56.420 --> 00:50:00.690
as you think about the
impact of AI and APIs

00:50:00.690 --> 00:50:03.590
and the duality of
it, think about some

00:50:03.590 --> 00:50:07.070
of the organization
structures that

00:50:07.070 --> 00:50:09.900
are fundamentally important.

00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:14.540
But the most important thing for
you is the theme that I always

00:50:14.540 --> 00:50:16.640
like to say, is we have--

00:50:16.640 --> 00:50:20.750
in the world of APIs, we
used to say, adapt or die.

00:50:20.750 --> 00:50:22.520
Sounds dangerous.

00:50:22.520 --> 00:50:25.640
I say adopt AI or die.

00:50:25.640 --> 00:50:27.440
So thank you very much, folks.

00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:32.180
And we have got some time for
questions, if you all have.

00:50:32.180 --> 00:50:34.850
And Jason, come on up.

00:50:34.850 --> 00:50:37.080
And let's see if there
are any questions.

00:50:37.080 --> 00:50:39.220
Otherwise, we'll be on our way.

00:50:44.530 --> 00:50:46.060
Going once?

00:50:46.060 --> 00:50:48.770
Twice?

00:50:48.770 --> 00:50:51.770
Thrice?

00:50:51.770 --> 00:50:52.730
Please, OK.

00:50:52.730 --> 00:50:57.030
Can you either use a mic
or all of you wait for my--

00:50:57.030 --> 00:50:59.855
OK, go ahead and say the
question, then I'll--

00:50:59.855 --> 00:51:01.980
AUDIENCE: Jason, how do
you monetize your business?

00:51:01.980 --> 00:51:04.072
What's your engagement
model with your clients?

00:51:04.072 --> 00:51:05.030
ANANT JHINGRAN: Repeat.

00:51:05.030 --> 00:51:07.820
JASON SMITH: Yeah,
so the question was

00:51:07.820 --> 00:51:09.570
how do we monetize our
business and what's

00:51:09.570 --> 00:51:11.400
our engagement model
with our clients?

00:51:11.400 --> 00:51:13.470
So we have a--

00:51:13.470 --> 00:51:15.660
it's essentially an
upfront license fee

00:51:15.660 --> 00:51:18.810
and an ongoing monthly fee
for insights and services.

00:51:18.810 --> 00:51:20.850
Traditionally, think of
it as enterprise SaaS

00:51:20.850 --> 00:51:22.357
as our primary right now.

00:51:22.357 --> 00:51:24.690
Over time, we'll have lower
costs for smaller companies.

00:51:24.690 --> 00:51:26.610
We're primarily focused
on larger players now,

00:51:26.610 --> 00:51:30.690
so our pharmaceutical partners,
our partnering with groups

00:51:30.690 --> 00:51:33.784
like University of Chicago and
some other investment firms,

00:51:33.784 --> 00:51:35.700
because that's really
the hands-on piece right

00:51:35.700 --> 00:51:36.908
now that we're involved with.

00:51:39.950 --> 00:51:41.180
ANANT JHINGRAN: OK.

00:51:41.180 --> 00:51:42.680
OK, great, guys.

00:51:42.680 --> 00:51:44.090
Thank you very, very much.

00:51:44.090 --> 00:51:45.857
And we are around
for some time in case

00:51:45.857 --> 00:51:47.190
you guys want to have questions.

00:51:47.190 --> 00:51:48.910
But otherwise,
thank you very much.

00:51:48.910 --> 00:51:50.710
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:50.710 --> 00:51:54.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

